course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 14
title: Your turn, I won, cheat! and tag
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every playground in the world has the same fights, and Ukrainian has the words: moya chErha! (my turn!), ya vyhRAv! (I won!), ty proHRAv (you lost) and the furious neCHEsno! (not fair!).
explain: ty shakhrUyesh! — you're cheating! — is stronger than neCHEsno and can start a real row, so it is worth being sure first.
explain: To start tag, kvach, or hide-and-seek, khovAnky, someone has to be «it»: ty vOdysh! — you're it! — and the usual answer is «ni, ty!».
explain: Hopscotch is klAsyky, chalked on the pavement, a skipping rope is a skAkalka, and m'yach is any ball.
fact: moya chErha! means my turn!, for example claiming the swing.
fact: ya vyhRAv! means I won!, said by a boy; a girl says ya vyhRAla, and the answer can be molodEts (well done) or neCHEsno!
fact: ty proHRAv means you lost; said gently it is fine, but gloating annoys, and a kind answer is nastupnoho razu, next time.
fact: neCHEsno! means that's not fair!; chesno means honestly or fairly.
fact: ty shakhrUyesh! means you're cheating!, stronger than neCHEsno and able to start a row; the reply is often ni, ne shakhruyu!
fact: ty vOdysh means you are «it», the one who catches or seeks; this vOdysh has nothing to do with vodA, water.
fact: kvach and kvAchyky mean tag, the chasing game; the one who tags shouts kvach! or ty kvach!
fact: khovAnky means hide-and-seek.
fact: zastukAv! is called in hide-and-seek when the seeker taps the base and names a player he has found; the base rules change from yard to yard, so ask first.
fact: klAsyky means hopscotch, drawn on the pavement with chalk.
fact: skAkalka means skipping rope.
fact: m'yach means ball; the apostrophe makes a little break, m-yach.
exercise: What does moya chErha! mean? | My turn! | My cherry! | Your turn! | My team!
exercise: What does ya vyhRAv! mean? | I won! | I lost! | I'm playing! | I'm tired!
exercise: What does a girl say for «I won»? | ya vyhRAla! | ya vyhRAv! | ya vyhRAlo! | ya vyhRAly!
exercise: What does ty proHRAv mean? | You lost | You won it | You're it | You cheated
exercise: What is a kind answer when you lose? | nastupnoho razu, next time | ty shakhrUyesh! you always cheat | neCHEsno, I'm off! | ya vyhRAv!
exercise: What does neCHEsno! mean? | That's not fair! | That's not true about me! | That's not tasty! | That's not mine!
exercise: What does chesno mean? | Honestly, fairly | Cheese, like cheddar | Quickly | Cheekily
exercise: Why be careful with ty shakhrUyesh!? | It is a strong accusation that can start a row | It is a swear word | It means you won | It means you're it
exercise: What does ty vOdysh mean? | You're «it», the one who catches or seeks | You're wet | You're the winner | You go first in the queue
exercise: What is kvach? | Tag, the chasing game | Hopscotch, on chalk squares | A ball | A skipping rope
exercise: What is khovAnky? | Hide-and-seek | Tag, the chasing game | Football in the yard | Hopscotch with chalk
exercise: When is zastukAv! called? | When the seeker taps the base and names a found player | When someone scores a goal | When the game is a draw | When someone falls over
exercise: Why ask about the rules of zastukAv! first? | The base rules change from yard to yard | It is illegal in some cities | Only girls may say it | It is a new game
exercise: What is klAsyky? | Hopscotch | Classes at school | Marbles | Skipping
exercise: What is a skAkalka? | A skipping rope | A skateboard with four wheels | A scooter | A swing
exercise: What does m'yach mean? | Ball | Match | Mitten | Me, myself
test: «ty vOdysh — like vodA, so you're wet??» Which is right? | No, it means you're «it» in the game | Yes, you're soaking wet from the rain | Yes, go and drink water, you're thirsty | No, it means you won
test: Sashko wins at cards and dances round the room chanting «ty proHRAv, ty proHRAv!». What is true? | Saying it is fine, but gloating like this annoys | It's very polite | It means «well done you» | It means «my turn»
test: Your friend saw the seeker peek. What would happen if he shouted «ty shakhrUyesh!»? | It could start a real row; it's a strong accusation | Everyone would laugh and clap | The seeker would win | Nothing, it means good luck
test: Sashko tags you and yells «ty kvach!». What now? | You're it — chase someone | You're out of the game | You've won | You must hide before he counts
test: «khovanky is tag» Which is right? | No, khovAnky is hide-and-seek; tag is kvach | Yes, they are the same game, just with two names | khovAnky is hopscotch | khovAnky is football
test: The seeker spots you behind the bins, runs to the base, taps it and shouts «zastukAv!» and your name. What happened? | He has found and «tapped you in» | He gave up | You won the game | It's a draw
test: A girl says «ya vyhRAv!». How would it normally be said by a girl? | ya vyhRAla! | ya vyhRAv! is right for girls | ya proHRAla | ya vyhRAvy
test: Kids are chalking squares and numbers on the pavement. What game is it? | klAsyky | kvach | khovAnky | futbOl
test: «oi! moya chErha!» shouts a boy at the swing. What does he want? | His turn on the swing | A cherry from the tree | To go home for his dinner | The ball you're holding
test: «that's neCHEsno, you moved twice» What is the kid saying? | That's not fair | That's not funny | That's not true at all | That's not mine, it's his
test: «chuck me the m'yach, mate» What does he want? | The ball | The skipping rope | The chalk | His turn
exam: Identify the expression employed to protest that the conduct of a game has been unjust. | neCHEsno! | ya vyhRAv! | moya chErha! | lovY!
exam: Select the response consistent with the established signification of ty vOdysh. | It designates the player who must catch or seek | It designates the player who has won the round outright | It is an injunction to drink water | It designates the player whose turn has passed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the accusation ty shakhrUyesh!? | It is more grave than neCHEsno! and may occasion a genuine quarrel | It is a gentle expression of praise | It is identical in force to molodEts | It signifies a draw
exam: Ascertain the game designated by the term klAsyky. | Hopscotch | Hide-and-seek | The game of tag | Football
